        "note": "The curve that leaves the smallest total of squared errors between it and the readings."
      },
      {
        "id": "normal",
        "label": "Setting the derivative to nothing",
        "focus": [
          "normal"
        ],
        "note": "That gives one square system: (A' * A) * c = A' * y, with a power of x in each column of A."
      },
      {
        "id": "cholesky",
        "label": "Two triangles, not one square",
        "focus": [
          "chol",
          "tri"
        ],
        "note": "The factor of Cholesky turns the square problem into two triangular ones, and a triangle is solved by substitution alone."
      },
      {
        "id": "centred",
        "label": "Why the places move",
        "focus": [
          "centre",
          "digits"
        ],
        "note": "A power of a large x runs out of digits quickly. Moving the places to sit about zero is what keeps the fit from failing."
      }
    ]
  },
